 "antecedent": "After these exchanges, the chief judge asks you to translate your high‑level concerns into a concrete statutory framework, focusing on how your proposed limits would apply in a scenario involving a credible threat to a crowded public event.",
 "perceived_simulation": "[Messages]\n[Moderator]: Thank you, counsel, for those opening statements. We will now turn to structured questioning by the panel, beginning with questions for the advocate who has urged substantial tightening or rejection of the draft framework.\n\n[Chief_judge]: Counsel, your remarks were helpful in distinguishing between documented and speculative harms, and in emphasizing distributional consequences. The panel would now like to understand more precisely how your proposed safeguards would function in practice.\n\n[Associate_judge_2]: To support that, the clerk has circulated a brief addendum summarizing several case studies from Tabs 5 and 6—both instances of wrongful identification and examples where timely identification appears to have prevented serious harm. We will be drawing on those as we ask both sides to walk through concrete scenarios.\n\n[Opposing_advocate]: If I may, Madam Chair, I want to acknowledge that my colleague’s emphasis on independent audits and strict limits on secondary use is entirely compatible with the framework I support. Where I part company is with the suggestion, implicit in some of the opposing submissions, that the tools should nearly always be sidelined in high‑risk environments; that, in my view, is an unduly cautious reading of what these case studies show about the costs of inaction.\n\n[Associate_judge_1]: Counsel, just a reminder to keep your critiques tied to the arguments rather than to characterizations of your colleague.\n\n[Opposing_advocate]: Of course, Your Honor. To be more precise: the record indicates that under certain, well‑specified conditions, these systems can prevent serious harm, and I worry that an overbroad moratorium would ignore that evidence.\n\n[Chief_judge]: Thank you. Turning back to you, counsel opposing the draft: you have advocated for statutory prohibitions on bulk collection and secondary uses, probable‑cause court orders for live queries, and an initial moratorium pending rigorous pilots. Imagine a jurisdiction facing a credible, time‑bounded threat to a large public event—say, a festival or transit hub—where there is specific intelligence about individuals of concern. How would you write the statute so that it permits narrowly targeted, time‑limited real‑time identification in such circumstances, while preventing the kind of persistent, population‑wide surveillance and downstream function creep that you have warned us about?",
 "response": "Write the statute to permit real‑time identification only by a probable‑cause court order from a neutral magistrate that explicitly names the individual(s) or narrowly circumscribed descriptors, defines precise geographic boundaries, and caps a short temporal window (e.g., ≤72 hours) tied to a particularized, time‑bound threat. \nInclude an operational ban on bulk or continuous population‑wide collection so systems may only run point‑in‑time queries against the court‑authorized watchlist; require automatic purge of all non‑matches immediately or within 24 hours and limit retention of matches to a fixed, brief period pending judicial review. \nStatutorily forbid secondary uses (e.g., immigration enforcement, commercial uses, or broad investigatory “chaining”) without a new probable‑cause order, and prohibit creation or expansion of persistent watchlists absent individualized probable cause and periodic judicial reauthorization. \nMandate pre‑deployment, independent accuracy and bias testing with public certification, comprehensive immutable logging with independent audit access, aggregate public reporting plus confidential after‑action reports to the authorizing court, and clear notice and remedy pathways to affected persons once disclosure would not jeopardize safety."
