 "antecedent": "The chief judge begins the questioning phase by asking you to spell out how your recommended safeguards would function in concrete scenarios, with particular attention to warrant standards and operational constraints in high‑risk settings.",
 "perceived_simulation": "[Messages]\n[Moderator]: Thank you, counsel, for those opening presentations. We will now move into a period of structured questioning from the panel, beginning with questions for the expert opposing the current draft framework and then turning to questions for the proponent.\n\n[Chief_judge]: Let me first say that I appreciate the way both of you have grounded your opening remarks in the record. Counsel, your presentation in opposition to the draft was notably specific about categories of risk, and I would like to test how your proposed safeguards would work on the ground.\n\n[Associate_judge_2]: To assist with that, the clerk has circulated a brief synthesis of the studies in Tabs 4 through 9, including misidentification rates and the conditions attached to the reported crime‑reduction benefits. We will be referring to that document in our questions.\n\n[Opposing_advocate]: If I may, Madam Chair, I want to acknowledge that my colleague’s emphasis on transparency, independent audits, and limits on secondary use aligns with elements my side also supports. Where we may differ is more about the calibration of thresholds and the scope of permissible deployments, not the legitimacy of those concerns themselves.\n\n[Chief_judge]: Thank you, counsel. That clarification is helpful and underscores that there is at least some shared foundation here.\n\n[Chief_judge]: Turning back to you, counsel opposing the draft: you have urged a moratorium on nationwide, untargeted deployment and proposed, among other things, a warrant requirement for targeted real‑time identification, strict retention limits, and independent audits. I would like you to walk us through how those constraints would operate in a concrete high‑risk scenario—say, a string of credible threats against a major transit hub—without either rendering the technology unusable or replicating the very risks you identified. In particular, what warrant standard, what temporal and geographic bounds, and what audit triggers would you recommend the statute specify for such cases?",
 "response": "Require a probable‑cause warrant narrowly particularized to the specific threat or person(s) and location, with a clear exigent‑use exception that mandates retrospective judicial approval within 48 hours. Limit operational scope to a short, specified temporal window (initial authorization no more than 72 hours; any extension only after the judge finds continued necessity and is narrowly tailored, with cumulative duration capped unless exceptional facts are found). Define geographic bounds in the warrant by precise coordinates and camera identifiers so only the minimum set of sensors covering the transit hub and immediate approaches are active (no city‑ or region‑wide sweeps). Mandate deletion of all non‑match biometric captures within 24 hours, permit retention of match results and narrow case files only while active under criminal‑procedure evidence rules (raw biometric templates may not be ingested into standing databases absent indictment), and require full immutable logging of queries, algorithm versions, and operator actions. Trigger automatic independent audits within 30 days of any deployment (and after any match leading to arrest), plus public transparency reports within 60 days and statutory civil remedies and criminal penalties for unauthorized or secondary uses."
