counsel supporting a more permissive framework. Please keep your remarks focused on concrete recommendations and limited to a few minutes.\n[Chief_judge]: Counsel, in your closing, would you briefly synthesize the key elements you believe any national framework should contain—on authorization, scope, safeguards, and adaptation over time—and explain, in light of the evidence, why those elements strike the right balance between enabling legitimate, well‑supervised uses and preventing the systemic rights risks you have described?",
 "response": "Any national framework should start with non‑negotiable statutory baselines: an explicit ban on untargeted, continuous real‑time biometric surveillance in public spaces and a probable‑cause, narrowly particularized warrant (with a documented exigency exception requiring retrospective judicial approval within 48 hours). Warrants must specify precise geographic coordinates and camera identifiers, be time‑limited (initial max 72 hours, extensions only upon fresh judicial findings), require deletion of non‑matches within 24–48 hours, and bar ingestion of raw biometric templates into standing databases absent indictment. Mandatory safeguards must include immutable, auditable logs of all queries and operator actions, independent accredited algorithmic and operational audits tied to deployments and arrests, public transparency reports, a statutory prohibition on secondary uses, and enforceable civil and criminal remedies for misuse. Expansion beyond pilots should be contingent on pre‑registered, court‑approved pilot protocols with independent evaluation against predefined metrics (demographic accuracy, false‑positive/negative rates, arrest/conviction yield, measures of chilling and disparate impact), plus sunset and mandatory review clauses before any broader authorization. This package preserves narrowly tailored, evidence‑based tools for genuine public‑safety needs while addressing the record’s clear demonstrations of bias, privacy invasion, chilling effects, and function‑creep risk."
 }
